\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Async - Await
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
Async/await is JavaScript's way of handling operations that take time - like reading from a database, fetching from an API, or reading a file. These operations don't complete instantly, so JavaScript nee\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
ds a way to wait for them without freezing your entire program.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Why we need it:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
When you query MongoDB, it takes time - maybe milliseconds, maybe longer if the network is slow. Without async/await, your code would try to use the data before it arrives, causing errors. JavaScript would move to the next line immediately, and you'd have
\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1  nothing to work with yet.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 What async does:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 When you put async before a function, you're telling JavaScript: "This function will do something that takes time, and it will return\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
 a Promise." A Promise is basically JavaScript's way of saying "I promise I'll give you a result eventually - either success or failure."
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 What await does:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
When you put await before an operation, you're telling JavaScript: "Stop here and wait for this to finish before moving to the next line." Without await, the code would keep running and you'd get a Promise instead of the actual data. await unwraps the Pro
\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 mise and gives you the actual result.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Why they work together:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid16595236 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 You can only use await inside an async f\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
unction. They're a pair. The async keyword sets up the function to handle waiting, and await does the actual waiting. If you try to use await in a regular function, JavaScript will throw an error.
\par 
